some super interesting primary results  from yesterday progressive activist  corey bush has defeated  10 term u.s congressman lacey clay  in the democratic primary in the first  district in missouri  and this is an interesting seat because  it has actually been  in the clay family since the 1960s bill  clay represented the district for 30  years  then his son william lacy clay  jr was elected in  so it's been since the 1960s that this  seat  has been in the same family in missouri  now back in 2018 corey bush ran against  lacy clay  and lost by 20 points not a small  defeat there for cory bush last night  she won roughly 51 to 49 percent  as of the latest numbers i saw corey  bush  is a nurse and an activist and this is a  pretty similar situation to aoc's  district  in new york city where the winner of the  democratic primary  is overwhelmingly likely to win the  general in november so essentially  the democratic primary is is really the  big election there is a republican  candidate it's anthony rogers he won the  republican primary  uh last night as well and it is you  might be  if you're not familiar with the missouri  first district you might be surprised to  hear that it is such a safe district for  for democrats  uh it's an extremely uh urban district  so as we know  in the united states there are voting  differences among  uh religions there are voting  differences among age voting differences  among race but the urban rural divide is  also  significant and this is a very urban  district and widely expected  to go to cory bush in a similar  situation as that which we saw  with aoc not long ago now  there's an important reminder here about  how you we can't really evaluate the  potential impact of a biden presidency  without taking into consideration the  house and senate now we've talked about  the senate before and with the senate  it's a much more obvious analysis which  is  if republicans can continue to control  the senate  during joe biden's at least first two  years in office if biden were to win  there's going to be a dramatically  smaller number  of even potential big progressive  victories if you have biden in the oval  office  a democratically controlled house and a  republican senate  it's going to be a fight it's going to  be obstruction and the amount that can  get done is going to be  much more limited if democrats can take  the senate  and keep the house and joe biden wins  you will have a two-year period during  which you could see  some pretty major progressive  advancements made they might not be  perfect they might  not be what bernie would do but they  would be significant and notable  advancements  in the progressive in the potentially  more progressive house  the effect is more nuanced because what  we're talking about in the house is it's  already democratically controlled every  expectation is that democrats will  retain control in the house of  representatives but  will it move to the left and when corey  bush replaces lacy  lacy clay that is a potential move  to the left for sure and that is a more  nuanced situation  will the bills that make it to biden's  desk  which he will be forced to sign or not  sign  and of course that would be after  conferencing with the senate assuming  democrats take it  a more progressive house also better  positions us  to put more progressive legislation in  front of joe biden  potentially getting him to sign it so  this is a really big deal  and if we do see uh these two years  of total control by democrats white  house  senate house we're very much getting  ahead of ourselves we're not going to  put the cart before the horse here  you could see one of the greatest  reversals in a two-year period  from the completely whacked and  outrageous white house of donald trump  with a republican senate that has gotten  so many judges  in appointed in confirmed for life  positions  you could actually see a really big  reversal uh to american politics  would it be what bernie would do if he  was given carte blanche no but that's  not the system we have we have a system  where we have a house  we have a senate uh we we have states  rights  we have a white house and it would be a  very very interesting situation now as  far as corey bush  she supports medicare for all she  supports a green new deal  supports 15 minimum wage wants public  funding of elections and takes no  corporate pac money so a very  interesting replacement to the uh uh  cur still incumbent as far as the house  is concerned  uh lacy clay this also this is  maybe one other angle worth touching on  this does suggest that aoc was not an  anomaly or an anomaly  both of course valid pronunciations and  that this may be a real wave  of replacing some of these more  entrenched legacy democrats  with younger more progressive  alternatives  the effect that they will have much like  in 2010 when the tea party  surfaced and won a bunch of seats  just because they were winning seats  didn't tell us what effect they would  have on the national politics or on the  republican party  we later saw that they certainly uh  became a notable voice in the republican  party and was sort of absorbed into it  now there's a tea party caucus  still an open question what the longer  term effect is  uh of these progressive victories in the  house and much of it will depend again  on the circumstances of who's in the  white house starting in january  and who controls the senate starting in  january and whether you do or don't like  any individual policy from any of these  progressive replacements to legacy  democrats that's  less important than the realization that  there is a changing of the guard  happening here  the i don't think that can be denied  anymore the open question that we will  have to answer over time  is what will the effect be on not just  the ideology of the country  but on the policy of the country as well
 

